>
  <p>
    Sudoku looks intimidating — a grid full of numbers — but it is one of the easiest puzzles to
    learn and one of the most satisfying to master. There is only a single rule, you never do any
    arithmetic, and every puzzle can be solved by reasoning alone. This guide takes you from a
    blank grid to confidently placing your first numbers. When you want to go further, our
    <a href="/guides/sudoku-solving-techniques/">advanced techniques guide</a> picks up where this
    one leaves off.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Sudoku?</h2>
  <p>
    Sudoku is a logic puzzle played on a <strong>9&times;9 grid</strong> — eighty-one squares in
    all. Some squares already contain numbers (the <strong>clues</strong>, or givens); the rest
    are blank, and your job is to fill them in. The digits 1 through 9 are used purely as nine
    different symbols, so <strong>no math is needed</strong>: you are not adding anything, just
    working out where each digit belongs. If you would rather see it in motion than read about it,
    you can <a href="/sudoku/">play a puzzle now</a> and follow along.
  </p>

  <h2>The one rule</h2>
  <p>
    Everything in Sudoku comes from a single rule: <strong>fill the grid so that every row, every
    column, and every 3&times;3 box contains the digits 1 to 9 exactly once.</strong> That is the
    whole game. To apply it, you need to picture the three kinds of group:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rows</strong> — the nine horizontal lines running left to right. Each row holds
      nine squares and must contain each digit 1&ndash;9 once.</li>
    <li><strong>Columns</strong> — the nine vertical lines running top to bottom. Same deal: every
      digit once, no repeats.</li>
    <li><strong>Boxes</strong> — the grid is divided by heavier lines into nine 3&times;3 blocks.
      Each box must also contain each digit exactly once.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because of this, a digit can never repeat within the same row, column or box. Every square sits
    at the crossing of one row, one column and one box, so it must satisfy all three at the same
    time — and that overlap is exactly what lets you deduce the answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting started</h2>
  <p>
    Begin by reading the clues already on the board — they are the foundation everything else is
    built on. Then look for squares whose value is <strong>forced</strong>: a square where only one
    digit can legally go, because every other digit already appears in its row, its column or its
    box. Filling one square often forces the next, and the puzzle unravels from there. New players
    do well to start on an <a href="/sudoku-easy/">easy puzzle</a>, where plenty of clues make
    these forced squares easy to spot.
  </p>

  <h2>The two beginner techniques</h2>
  <p>
    Almost every easy puzzle can be finished with just two ideas, both found by <strong>scanning
    </strong> — sweeping your eye along rows, columns and boxes to see what is missing.
  </p>
  <h3>Naked single</h3>
  <p>
    A <strong>naked single</strong> is a square that has only one possible digit left. Look at an
    empty square and check its row, column and box. If eight of the nine digits already appear
    somewhere in those three groups, the ninth digit is the only one that fits — so it must go
    there. The answer is &ldquo;naked&rdquo; because there is nothing to hide behind.
  </p>
  <h3>Hidden single</h3>
  <p>
    A <strong>hidden single</strong> works the other way around. Pick a digit and a single group —
    say, a box — and ask where that digit can possibly go inside it. If every empty square but one
    is blocked (because the digit already sits in that square&rsquo;s row or column), then the one
    remaining square must take the digit, even if that square could in theory hold other numbers
    too. Scanning one digit at a time across all nine boxes is the fastest way to find these.
  </p>

  <h2>Pencil marks and candidates</h2>
  <p>
    When scanning stops turning up easy placements, switch to <strong>pencil marks</strong> (also
    called notes or candidates). For each empty square you write small the digits that could still
    legally go there. With the candidates laid out, naked singles jump out as squares with only one
    note, and you can spot patterns you would miss in your head. On CardHearth, toggle
    <strong>Notes</strong> (the &#10070; button, or press <strong>P</strong>) and the board keeps
    your candidates tidy — placing a real digit automatically erases that candidate from every
    square it rules out.
  </p>

  <h2>The CardHearth promise: no guessing, ever</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part that matters most for beginners. <strong>Every CardHearth Sudoku has a single
    unique solution and is solvable by pure logic — you never have to guess.</strong> Each puzzle
    is generated and then verified by a solver before you ever see it, at every difficulty from
    Easy to Expert. If you ever feel stuck, the answer is to look harder for a deduction, not to
    take a gamble: a logical step is always available. For more on how the clue counts and
    deduction lengths change as puzzles get harder, see our
    <a href="/guides/sudoku-difficulty-levels/">Sudoku difficulty levels guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Tips for beginners</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Start with the most-filled groups.</strong> The row, column or box with the fewest
      blanks is the easiest place to find a forced square.</li>
    <li><strong>Scan one digit at a time.</strong> Pick a digit that already appears often and hunt
      for hidden singles across the boxes.</li>
    <li><strong>Pencil in candidates once scanning stalls</strong> — and keep them current as you
      place digits.</li>
    <li><strong>Never guess.</strong> If nothing is obvious, the deduction is just hiding; re-check
      your notes rather than trying a number to &ldquo;see what happens.&rdquo;</li>
    <li><strong>Use undo freely.</strong> On CardHearth undo is unlimited, so exploring is safe.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Once these basics feel natural and you move on to Hard and Expert puzzles, you will meet longer
    deduction chains that need a few more tools — pairs, pointing, X-Wings and the like. Our
    <a href="/guides/sudoku-solving-techniques/">Sudoku solving techniques guide</a> walks through
    each of them in order.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
